    "result_text": "I've implemented `publish_run` in `app/publish.py`. All 3 tests pass: the existing one plus 2 new ones. Nothing has been run against the real Hub.\n\n**One thing to know first:** I couldn't use Context7 for the docs lookup that CLAUDE.md asks for, because it returned \"Monthly quota exceeded\". Instead I read the source of the installed huggingface_hub 2.0.0. That mattered: `upload_large_folder`, the usual way to upload very large folders, **doesn't exist in 2.0.0**. In this version, `upload_folder` does that job itself when `hf_xet` is installed. According to its docstring:\n- it splits large folders into several commits, so the number of files doesn't make it fail;\n- if an upload is interrupted, running the same call again skips files that are already committed and doesn't re-send data that was already uploaded.\n\n`hf_xet` 1.6.0 is already in `.venv`, and huggingface_hub installs it by default on x86_64 and arm64, so no new dependency was needed.\n\n**What `publish_run` does:**\n- **Repo:** calls `create_repo(..., repo_type=\"dataset\", private=True, exist_ok=True)`. This creates the repo as private if it's missing and does nothing if it already exists.\n- **Upload:** calls `upload_folder` at the repo root with `ignore_patterns=[\"*.tmp\", \"wandb/\"]`. The library turns `wandb/` into `wandb/*`, and its `*` also matches `/`. So `*.tmp` is skipped at any depth, and everything under the top-level `wandb/` is skipped.\n- **Safety check:** if `hf_xet` isn't installed, it raises a `RuntimeError` right away. Without `hf_xet`, `upload_folder` quietly falls back to a single commit that can't be resumed, which would break both of your large-folder requirements.\n- **Test hook:** I added an optional `api` parameter so tests can pass in a fake client, the same pattern `publish_summary` uses.\n\n**Tests** (`tests/test_publish.py`):\n- One checks the arguments passed to `create_repo` and `upload_folder`.\n- The other runs sample paths through the library's own pattern filter (`filter_repo_objects`)"
  },
